window.docContents[136].resolve({"/References/#Type-Systems--References___-Typing-Mutable-References--Properties--Preservation--Weakening-for-Stores":{"contents":"Finally, we need a lemma on store typings, stating that, if a\nstore typing is extended with a new location, the extended one\nstill allows us to assign the same types to the same terms as the\noriginal.\n\ntheorem store_weakening {Γ : Context} {δ δ' : StoreTy} {t : Tm} {τ : Ty}\n  (hExt : Extends δ' δ)\n  (hTy : <{ Γ / δ ⊢ t ⦂ τ }>) :\n  <{ Γ / δ' ⊢ t ⦂ τ }> := by\n    induction hTy with (try (apply_rules using StlcRefTyping; done))\n    | loc_ h =>\n        rw [←extends_lookup] <;> try assumption\n        apply HasType.loc_\n        apply length_extends <;> assumption\n\n\nWe can use the store_weakening lemma to prove that if a store is\nwell typed with respect to a store typing, then the store extended\nwith a new term τ will still be well typed with respect to the\nstore typing extended with τ's type.\n\ntheorem store_well_typed_app {δ : StoreTy} {ρ : Store} {t₁ : Tm} {τ₁ : Ty}\n  (hStoreTy : δ ⊢ ρ)\n  (hTy : <{ ∅ / δ ⊢ t₁ ⦂ τ₁ }>) :\n  (δ.concat τ₁) ⊢ (ρ.concat t₁) := by\n    have ⟨h₁, h₂⟩ := hStoreTy\n    simp_all only [List.concat_eq_append]\n    constructor\n    · simp_all\n    · simp_all only [List.length_append, List.length_cons, List.length_nil, Nat.zero_add]\n      intro l hl\n      inversion hl with\n      | refl =>\n          apply store_weakening <;> try assumption\n          apply extends_app\n          simp_all [MyGetElem.getElem]\n      | step hl =>\n          apply store_weakening <;> try assumption\n          · apply extends_app\n          · have hl' : l < ρ.length := by lia\n            simp_all [MyGetElem.getElem, List.getElem?_append_left]\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009References: Typing Mutable References\u0009Properties\u0009Preservation","header":"9.5.4.3. Weakening for Stores","id":"/References/#Type-Systems--References___-Typing-Mutable-References--Properties--Preservation--Weakening-for-Stores"},"/Sub/#Type-Systems--Sub___-Subtyping--Formal-Definitions--Subtyping":{"contents":"Now we come to the interesting part.  We begin by defining\nthe subtyping relation and developing some of its important\ntechnical properties.The definition of subtyping is just what we sketched in the\nmotivating discussion.\n\nsection\nset_option hygiene false in\nlocal notation:40 τ:41 \" <: \" τ':41 => Subtype τ τ'\n\ninductive Subtype : Ty → Ty → Prop where\n  | refl {τ : Ty} :\n      τ <: τ\n  | trans {σ υ τ: Ty}\n      (h₁ : σ <: υ)\n      (h₂ : υ <: τ) :\n      σ <: τ\n  | top {σ : Ty} :\n      σ <: <{ ⊤ }>\n  | arrow { σ₁ σ₂ τ₁ τ₂ : Ty}\n      (h₁ : τ₁ <: σ₁)\n      (h₂ : σ₂ <: τ₂) :\n      <{ σ₁ → σ₂ }> <: <{ τ₁ → τ₂ }>\n\n-- Fill in more rules when you do the `products` exercise\n  | prod { σ₁ σ₂ τ₁ τ₂ : Ty}\n      (h₁ : σ₁ <: τ₁)\n      (h₂ : σ₂ <: τ₂) :\n      <{ σ₁ × σ₂ }> <: <{ τ₁ × τ₂ }>\nend\n\nscoped notation:40 τ:41 \" <: \" τ':41 => Subtype τ τ'\n\nattribute [StlcSubTyping] Subtype.refl Subtype.trans Subtype.top\n    Subtype.arrow\nSubtype.prod\n\n\n\n\nNote that we don't need any special rules for base types (Bool\nand Base): they are automatically subtypes of themselves (by\nrefl) and ⊤ (by top), and that's all we want.\n\nnamespace Examples\n\nexample : <{ C → Bool }> <: <{ C → ⊤ }> := by\n  solve_by_elim using StlcSubTyping\nNote that, because the Subtype rules are not \"syntax directed\"\n(e.g., given a goal of the form ⊤ <: ⊤, you could apply the top rule,\nthe refl rule, the trans rule), we have to use solve_by_elim here\ninstead of apply_rules.Potentially we could introduce the syntax-directed version of the subtyping judgment\nhere as a fix for this.Leave this exercise until after you have finished adding product\ntypes to the language — see exercise products — at least up to\nthis point in the file.Recall that, in chapter MoreStlc, the optional section\n\"Encoding Records\" describes how records can be encoded as pairs.\nUsing this encoding, define pair types representing the following\nrecord types:Person := { name : String }\nStudent := { name : String ; gpa : Float }\nEmployee := { name : String ; ssn : Integer }\ndef person : Ty := solution!(<{ String × ⊤ }>)\ndef student : Ty := solution!(<{ String × (⊤ × Float) }>)\ndef employee : Ty := solution!(<{ String × (Integer × ⊤) }>)\nNow use the definition of the subtype relation to prove the following:example : student <: person := by\n  solution!\n    rw [student, person]; solve_by_elim using StlcSubTyping\n\nexample : employee <: person := by\n  solution!\n    rw [employee, person]; solve_by_elim using StlcSubTyping\nThe following facts are mostly easy to prove in Lean.  To get\nfull benefit from the exercises, make sure you also\nunderstand how to prove them on paper!example : <{ ⊤ → student }> <:  <{ (C → C) → person }> := by\n  solution!\n    rw [student, person]; solve_by_elim using StlcSubTyping\nexample : <{ ⊤ → person }> <: <{ person → ⊤ }> := by\n  solution!\n    rw [person]; solve_by_elim using StlcSubTyping\nend Examples\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Sub: Subtyping\u0009Formal Definitions","header":"8.2.2. Subtyping","id":"/Sub/#Type-Systems--Sub___-Subtyping--Formal-Definitions--Subtyping"}});